show business is notoriously fickle some  actors are lucky enough to have careers  that last decades whereas  others struggle to capitalize on early  success if you're curious as to what  happened to the ones who fit in the  latter group keep watching to find out  robert duncan mcneil owes a lot to star  trek one of his most notable roles was  as starfleet cadet nicholas lecarno on  the next generation episode the first  duty he then became cocky helmsman tom  paris on voyager which would become his  defining role he would ultimately phase  acting out of his life and replace it  with directing a skill that he learned  over multiple trek series  on his first day shooting the voyager  pilot mcneil approached producer rick  berman and expressed interest in  directing an episode as soon as possible  he'd long wanted to direct but the shows  he'd acted in kept getting canceled so  instead of waiting around this time  he wanted a chance to direct during  voyager's first season he ultimately got  his chance with season three's  sacred ground and he would go on to  direct three more episodes of voyager  when that show ended he took a few small  acting roles and then transitioned to  full-time directing and producing with  his credits ranging from such shows as  the oc  chuck and blue bloods franco  zepharelli's 1968 version of romeo and  juliet was a huge hit at the time  and still endures as one of the best  shakespeare adaptations ever  it was especially notable for casting  actual teenagers as the title characters  with olivia hussey as juliet  and leonard whiting as romeo both earned  praise for their performances and were  considered future stars  hussey would indeed go on to have a  respectable career but whiting all but  vanished the actor became a victim of  typecasting  as he played a few romantic leads in  smaller movies before becoming jaded he  took a break from acting and tried his  hand at writing  painting a series of books and plays  that never got published he lived off of  film residuals and the odd voice acting  gig or art picture  interestingly enough in 2015 whiting and  hussey reunited on screen in social  suicide which was a bit of a social  media age retelling of romeo and juliet  despite concerns about typecasting  almost every actor who's played james  bond has gone on to have a solid career  outside of their work in espionage  george lasenby however is the exception  the australian actor played bond in  1969's on her majesty's secret service  only to outright vanish from hollywood  after the movie hit theaters  however he regrets nothing lazenby was a  model by trade with no acting experience  who bluffed his way through the audition  to get the part  unfortunately during production he spent  much of his time clashing with bond  producer cubby broccoli which soured his  experience  nonetheless lazenby was offered a  million dollar contract for six more  movies but his agent convinced him not  to take it for two reasons  first he believed that spy characters  like bond were on their way out  second he believed it wouldn't be hard  for his client to find more million  dollar roles  and then my manager was telling me that  james bond was over  that you know it was make peace not love  not lazenby announced that he was  stepping down from the part before on  her majesty's secret service even  premiered  he went on to act in minor roles but he  never landed any of those big parts his  agent forecasted in 2017 he told usa  today  i'm glad i didn't do another bond  because i would have been totally  trapped in the film industry and i  wouldn't have had the life i had  i wouldn't have had the kids i had i'm  quite satisfied with the way my life has  turned out so far  while you might not recognize the name  peter ostrom you almost certainly know  his face  as a teenager he played charlie bucket  the beating heart of 1971's willy wonka  and the chocolate factory  i think hope i just won more my grandpa  joe  he performed the role with so much  curiosity and joy that we're still  talking about it today  alas though he never cashed that golden  ticket in for long-lasting stardom  instead he disappeared for a while  which is just how he wanted it after  willy wonka ostrom was offered a  three-picture deal but he turned it down  and eventually left acting behind  inspired by a veterinarian who took care  of his family's horse  he decided to become a vet himself he  got his degree from cornell university  and he works as a large animal  veterinarian in upstate new york to this  day  am i famous in my eyes absolutely not  and i'm  amused that everybody's here to talk to  me  and amazed ostrom has been reluctant to  talk about playing charlie for years  he didn't even tell his future wife that  he was in the movie until she was about  to meet his parents but around 1990 he  started going to a local school one day  a year to answer questions from kids  he also opened up publicly in 2005 when  tim burton's charlie and the chocolate  factory was released  and he's since taken part in several  willy wonka related promos and  screenings  jennifer gray had a small but memorable  role as the sister of the title  character in 1986's ferris bueller's day  off before starring as francis baby  houseman the following year in dirty  dancing  that role earned her fame acclaim and a  golden globe nomination but it's  impossible to talk about gray's rise  without also talking about her  subsequent fall while her co-star  patrick swayze's star only grew  gray hit a wall in 1990 she got a nose  job to remove a visible bump this  drastically changed her appearance and  without that distinctive feature  her roles dried up she called it the  nose job from hell and once said i went  into the operating room a celebrity and  came out anonymous  gray has gotten plenty of work in the  ensuing years including a recurring role  as herself in the late 90s sitcom  it's like you know but she's yet to land  another big role  she's still grateful for her part in  dirty dancing and the joy it brings  people but that doesn't mean it's not  repetitive at an anniversary event for  the film she joked i wish anytime i went  into a nice restaurant and asked for a  table they said well i'm  sure you don't want one in the corner  nobody puts baby in a corner  avery brooks played benjamin cisco on  all seven seasons of star trek deep  space nine  going from the commander of a backwater  post to a man on par with any admiral in  starfleet though it was never quite the  ratings hit as the next generation or  voyager  deep space nine is now regarded as one  of the best trek series cisco was unique  among star trek leads besides being the  first black lead character  he was more emotional and a more obvious  misfit than the average starfleet  officer  brook stands in stark contrast to his  fellow star trek captains and commanders  while most remain active in showbiz to  this day he stepped away from the  limelight as he took some time to raise  his daughters and became a professor at  rutgers university  he's taken on similar movie roles but he  primarily performs in theater he never  landed a big on-screen role again but he  doesn't seem to want one  and he doesn't give many interviews  though by all accounts he seems happy  with where his life is now  the wires proposition joe as played by  robert f chu  stood in stark contrast to the show's  other drug lords he was far more relaxed  verbose and manipulative he was also one  of the show's more sympathetic  characters despite ostensibly being one  of the bad guys he's one of just a  handful of street level characters who  appeared in all five seasons but after  the wire ended  chu only had two minor movie parts and  disappeared from public view  entirely by his own choice he lived his  whole life in baltimore the city where  the wire took place  he was born there he went to college  there and he died there he was a fixture  of the charm cities theater scene for a  few decades he also found time for some  tv work including small parts on wire  creator david simon's previous shows  homicide life on the street and the  corner  during his time on the wire he taught  acting at the arena players youth  theater in fact he got 22 of his  students roles on the show and he  coached many of the series new and  inexperienced actors  after the show concluded chu could have  moved to new york or los angeles and  found even greater success in hollywood  instead he stayed in baltimore and  continued to teach acting the city was  too important to him and he stayed there  and helped people hone their craft until  his death in january 2013.  in the early 90s eric de rey played leo  johnson  the first true villain of twin peaks as  an abusive husband  and the most obvious suspect in the  death of laura palmer leo terrified the  town and had every member of the  audience waiting for his comeuppance  i don't know he's the obvious one deray  could have had a long career in show  business  after twin peaks instead he vanished and  it's not entirely clear why he was born  into a showbiz family his father aldo  ray was a go-to tough guy actor for  decades and his mother joanna ray is a  casting director who's worked  extensively with david lynch the creator  of twin peaks after twin peaks was  cancelled deray spent years playing  smaller roles and obscure movies before  outright vanishing from hollywood around  2002.  he hasn't made a movie since then and he  rarely makes public appearances  in 2012 he did briefly resurface though  when he was wrongfully suspected of  starting fires in los angeles  zach galligan burst onto the scene in  1984 as billy peltzer in the hit movie  gremlins his performance proved his  value as a young actor with plenty of  potential  but despite that massive early success  he's done little of note since then  gallagher knew early on that executive  producer steven spielberg's involvement  meant the movie would be a hit an  assumption that proved to be correct  within three weeks of release he was  recognized worldwide everywhere from  local restaurants all the way to south  africa  after gremlins gallaghan went to college  at columbia university  after school he moved back to los  angeles hoping to restart his acting  career but those four years away had  really sapped his momentum he narrowly  missed getting cast in such movies as  saint elmo's fire the rocketeer  and platoon he was able to reprise the  part of billy in 1990s gremlins 2 the  new batch  which bombed in its original release but  has since become a cult classic  he spent his time since then still  acting as well as writing and teaching  acting he's never quite found that next  big role but given how his life has  turned out he thinks that may  actually have been for the best dan  lloyd was unforgettable as little danny  torrance in the 1980 horror classic the  shiner though he never found the role  all that scary  audiences may jump at danny riding his  bike up to those creepy twins but off  screen he played games with the actual  twins between takes  come and play with us even when lloyd  watched the movie years later he wasn't  horrified  instead he was reminded of all the fun  he had on set it was his one big role  before disappearing from show business  but don't worry about him as he's doing  just fine  lloyd tried out for a few more roles  after the shining but he had trouble  landing anything so he just left the  business entirely he now teaches biology  at a community college in kentucky where  he learned the hard way not to tell his  students about his past  lest they disrupt class with chance of  red rum he doesn't give many interviews  about his acting days though he has  revealed that he loved it then and he's  happy with his life now and in 2019 he  had a cameo in the shining  sequel dr sleep check out one of our  newest videos right here plus more  looper videos about your favorite stuff  are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  you

